# ⚠️ ⚠️ **NEVER PUT A REAL KEY IN THIS FILE.** It is **committed**, so a key pasted here is a key *published* — which is
# why the values below are deliberately empty, and why `.gitignore` keeps `.env` out of the tree. ⚠️ **Copy it first:**
#
#   cp .env.example .env        then fill in the values in `.env`
#
# ⚠️ **Read at the start of every run** by `src/market_overview/env.py`, and **only where the environment does not
# already set the variable** — so an exported value always wins, and one key can be overridden for one run without
# touching this file. ⚠️ The format is `KEY=VALUE`, one per line; blank lines, `#` comments and a leading `export `
# are all fine, and a line that is not a pair is skipped rather than fatal.

# The New York Times Books API — ⚠️ **required by the `english` and `comics` charts.**
#
# ⚠️ **UNCOMMENT THE LINE BELOW AND PASTE YOUR KEY AFTER THE `=`** — in `.env`, never here. A line beginning with `#` is
# not read at all, and an empty value (`NYT_API_KEY=`) is treated as *unset*: either one leaves both charts absent with
# the message `NYT_API_KEY is not set`, which is the whole failure this file exists to prevent.
#
# ⚠️ A run with no key makes the charts **absent** rather than failing: `fetch.nyt` raises `MissingKeyError`, and the
# conductor treats a vendor that did not answer as a chart that does not ship (ticket 18) — so the run refuses only if
# *nothing* could be built.
#
# Free and self-serve: developer.nytimes.com → Apps → + New App → ⚠️ **enable the Books API**, or every call is a 401.
# ⚠️ Read the Terms of Use and the Attribution Guidelines there before publishing a chart to YouTube.
# NYT_API_KEY=

# Google Books — ⚠️ **optional.** It only raises the quota on the cover and enrichment lookups; without it the product
# uses the anonymous quota, which is usually enough for one week and not for a backfill.
# GOOGLE_BOOKS_API_KEY=
